{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le xy}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/6b3720af66e81aef6792d4b2a34b2bdf2b93bd59ed6e28e4b38a1e06964bb2cc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/9dc99cc0df9f089b9f62402c3c5ea9c4408be077debbf6dfadeb884ee4d8b2d0.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[z]_{0}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[{x}^{2}]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{4})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ce7a4498912f0417209218ae7e6edd946f4d1a48ee3035e32149a1d10f5e043"} {"question" "q-162236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1"] ["math" "\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/38eb7316b1ee5c947eee37a9cc10530ff08f793b7564cf0d88c6ce24e1239f9d.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/241abff7fae5d6ba3301d605ff4c4e62ff61e423515c7bc3a54b17b9aec525aa.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[z]_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-(1-e) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ce7a4498912f0417209218ae7e6edd946f4d1a48ee3035e32149a1d10f5e043"} {"question" "q-162250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2e+1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "e-1"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi /2}"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{{e}^{z}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{p}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,z)}^{{u}_{4}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le {e}^{z},\\,\\,{z}^{2}-1\\le y\\le x+z}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{{e}^{z}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/41b06d98498273a03b9df8eeaa60f4e74cb00168e8dd0b3b395c5b0a23518e57.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le {e}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/07a37b90df8b37d07c40af03ecba8e665797cfff368758db7ae17e45f11683cf.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=0\\,$ and $\\,z={x}^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,$ and $\\,z=1.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ce7a4498912f0417209218ae7e6edd946f4d1a48ee3035e32149a1d10f5e043"} {"question" "q-162322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "p=2x,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={e}^{x}"] ["math" "p=2x,\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r={e}^{x}"] ["math" "p=\\sqrt{x},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=x"] ["math" "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{(x+1)^{3}}^{2-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{p}{\\int }_{q}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}-1}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{r}^{0}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,z)}^{{u}_{4}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,(x+1)^{3}\\le z\\le 2-{x}^{2},\\,\\,x+z\\le y\\le z+2}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{(x+1)^{3}}^{2-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/5f586edef4202e3f3de09fe947c955de62efa35e421e85b3e5034efd375790a7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,(x+1)^{3}\\le z\\le 2-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/ec9845e347959ff1ac43a12d03261b1eb31172f14b56269d7bbfcb86a2a23e48.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{z}-1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-\\sqrt{2-z}\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,-1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt{2-z}\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}-1}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-z}}^{0}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ce7a4498912f0417209218ae7e6edd946f4d1a48ee3035e32149a1d10f5e043"} {"question" "q-162323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=-1,\\,\\,\\,r=-\\sqrt{2-z}"] ["math" "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=-z,\\,\\,\\,r=-\\sqrt{z-1}"] ["math" "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=-z,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2"] ["math" "p=2,\\,\\,\\,q=-1,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2"] ["math" "p=2,\\,\\,\\,q=-2,\\,\\,\\,r=-\\sqrt{2-z}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=-1,\\,\\,\\,r=-\\sqrt{2-z}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,z\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/54d8420870ea92e42811a13ed2c4563c5c9ccb674ee8c3b6a277186c0fba21da.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,z\\le x\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=z\\,⟹\\,z=x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/7f19b63139a3d38c321e9938c1e8fbbf84e924df51d9b9b057d0151fc269550d.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[z{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}z[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}z({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1){\\int }_{0}^{x}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1){[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[e-1-1+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ce7a4498912f0417209218ae7e6edd946f4d1a48ee3035e32149a1d10f5e043"} {"question" "q-162325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2e"] ["math" "4(e-1)"] ["math" "\\frac{e-2}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"} {"question" "q-162325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e-2}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a001c36ac3e7cbf3f0be07967d8492dd603d470072cd1e89aee5656dcc18589"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2839 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
